<h1>HTML Structure and Semantic Elements</h1>
<p>Learn the basic structure of HTML and how semantic elements organize web content.</p>
</header>
<!-- HTML INTRODUCTION -->
<section class="chapter" id="html-introduction">
<h2>HTML Introduction</h2>
<p>
                HTML is the foundation of web development.
                It provides the structure used to organize
                text, images, links, and other content on websites.
            </p>
</section>
<!-- WHAT IS HTML -->
<section class="chapter" id="what-is-html">
<h2>What is HTML?</h2>
<p>
                HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.
                It is the standard language used to structure
                content displayed in web browsers.
            </p>
<ul>
<li>HTML defines the structure of web pages.</li>
<li>HTML uses elements to organize content.</li>
<li>HTML elements are represented by tags.</li>
<li>Browsers interpret HTML to display web pages.</li>
<li>HTML identifies headings, paragraphs, links, and images.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="chapter" id="simple-html-document">
<h2>A Simple HTML Document</h2>
<p>
                An HTML document contains elements that define
                the page's structure and visible content.
            </p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
            &lt;html lang="en"&gt;
            &lt;head&gt;
                &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
                &lt;title&gt;My First Website&lt;/title&gt;
            &lt;/head&gt;
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Website&lt;/h1&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Learning HTML is fun!&lt;/p&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
            &lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Example Explained</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code>
                    declares the HTML document type.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;html&gt;</code>
                    is the root element of the document.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;head&gt;</code>
                    contains metadata and document settings.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;title&gt;</code>
                    sets the title displayed in the browser tab.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;body&gt;</code>
                    contains the visible page content.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>
                    defines a primary heading.
                </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;p&gt;</code>
                    defines a paragraph.
                </li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- HTML ELEMENTS -->
<section class="chapter" id="what-is-html-element">
<h2>What is an HTML Element?</h2>
<p>
                An HTML element usually consists of an opening tag,
                content, and a closing tag.
            </p>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;

            &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<h3>Parts of an HTML Element</h3>
<table class="lesson-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Opening Tag</th>
<th>Content</th>
<th>Closing Tag</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>&lt;h1&gt;</td>
<td>Hello World</td>
<td>&lt;/h1&gt;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&lt;p&gt;</td>
<td>This is a paragraph.</td>
<td>&lt;/p&gt;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&lt;br&gt;</td>
<td>No content</td>
<td>Not required</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>
<strong>Note:</strong> Some elements, such as
                <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> and
                <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, are void elements.
                They do not require closing tags.
            </p>
</section>
<!-- WEB BROWSERS -->
<section class="chapter" id="web-browsers">
<h2>Web Browsers</h2>
<p>
                A web browser is an application that reads HTML
                documents and displays them as web pages.
            </p>
<p>Popular web browsers include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Google Chrome</li>
<li>Mozilla Firefox</li>
<li>Microsoft Edge</li>
<li>Safari</li>
<li>Opera</li>
</ul>
<p>
                Browsers interpret HTML elements and render
                the content without displaying the HTML tags
                themselves.
            </p>
<h3>Browser Output Example</h3>
<div class="browser-preview">
<h3>Welcome to My Website</h3>
<p>Learning HTML is fun!</p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- HTML PAGE STRUCTURE -->
<section class="chapter" id="html-page-structure">
<h2>HTML Page Structure</h2>
<p>
                    Every HTML document follows a basic structure.
                    The head contains document information, while
                    the body contains the content users can see.
                </p>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;

        &lt;head&gt;
            &lt;title&gt;Page Title&lt;/title&gt;
        &lt;/head&gt;

        &lt;body&gt;

            &lt;h1&gt;Main Heading&lt;/h1&gt;

            &lt;p&gt;First paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;

            &lt;p&gt;Second paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;

        &lt;/body&gt;

    &lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
<strong>Remember:</strong> Content inside the
                    <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is rendered on the page,
                    while the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> identifies
                    the document in the browser tab.
                </p>
</section>
<!-- HTML HISTORY -->
<section class="chapter" id="html-history">
<h2>HTML History</h2>
<p>
                    HTML has developed through several versions
                    since the creation of the World Wide Web.
                    Each major update introduced improvements
                    to web document structure and capabilities.
                </p>
<table class="lesson-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Year</th>

<th>Development</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1989</td>

<td>Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1991</td>

<td>HTML was introduced for creating web documents.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1993</td>

<td>Dave Raggett proposed HTML+.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1995</td>

<td>HTML 2.0 became a formal specification.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1997</td>

<td>HTML 3.2 became a W3C Recommendation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1999</td>

<td>HTML 4.01 was published.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2000</td>

<td>XHTML 1.0 was released.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2008</td>

<td>The first public HTML5 draft was published by WHATWG.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2012</td>

<td>WHATWG adopted the HTML Living Standard.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2014</td>

<td>HTML5 became a W3C Recommendation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2016</td>

<td>HTML 5.1 reached Candidate Recommendation status.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2017</td>

<td>HTML 5.1 Second Edition and HTML 5.2 were published.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>
                    Modern HTML continues to evolve through the
                    HTML Living Standard, which supports the
                    development of today's websites and web applications.
                </p>
</section>
