<!-- HTML SEMANTIC ELEMENTS -->
<section class="chapter" id="html-semantic-elements">
<h2>HTML Semantic Elements</h2>
<p>
            Semantic elements are HTML elements that clearly
            describe the purpose and meaning of their content.
            They help developers and web browsers understand
            how different parts of a webpage are organized.
        </p>
<p>
            HTML provides semantic elements that make web pages
            more meaningful, readable, and easier to maintain.
        </p>
</section>
<!-- WHAT ARE SEMANTIC ELEMENTS? -->
<section class="chapter" id="what-are-semantic-elements">
<h2>What are Semantic Elements?</h2>
<p>
            A semantic element is an HTML element whose name
            communicates the type or purpose of the content
            it contains.
        </p>
<p>
            Semantic elements describe their meaning to both
            the web browser and the developer.
        </p>
<h3>Non-Semantic Elements</h3>
<p>
            Non-semantic elements do not explain what their
            content represents. They are commonly used as
            general-purpose containers.
        </p>
<ul>
<li>
<code>&lt;div&gt;</code> -
                A general container used to group HTML elements.
            </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;span&gt;</code> -
                An inline container used to group text or
                other inline content.
            </li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of Non-Semantic HTML</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;div class="header"&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class="content"&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Welcome to my website.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
            Although these elements can organize content,
            their tag names do not identify the purpose
            of each section.
        </p>
<h3>Semantic Elements</h3>
<p>
            Semantic elements provide meaningful information
            about the content they contain.
        </p>
<ul>
<li>
<code>&lt;img&gt;</code> -
                Represents an image.
            </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;table&gt;</code> -
                Represents tabular data.
            </li>
<li>
<code>&lt;article&gt;</code> -
                Represents independent, self-contained content.
            </li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of Semantic HTML</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;header&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;My Website&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;/header&gt;

    &lt;main&gt;
        &lt;article&gt;
            &lt;h2&gt;Welcome&lt;/h2&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;This is my first article.&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;/article&gt;
    &lt;/main&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
            In this example, the elements identify the
            page header, main content, and an independent
            article.
        </p>
</section>
<!-- SEMANTIC ELEMENTS IN HTML -->
<section class="chapter" id="semantic-elements-in-html">
<h2>Semantic Elements in HTML</h2>
<p>
            Many websites previously relied on generic
            containers with IDs or classes to identify
            different parts of a webpage.
        </p>
<h3>Example of Traditional HTML Structure</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;div id="header"&gt;
        Website Header
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id="nav"&gt;
        Navigation Menu
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id="content"&gt;
        Main Content
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id="footer"&gt;
        Website Footer
    &lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
            Modern HTML provides semantic elements that
            describe the different areas of a webpage
            more clearly.
        </p>
<h3>Common HTML Semantic Elements</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>&lt;article&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;aside&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;details&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;figure&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;footer&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;header&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;mark&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;section&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;summary&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>&lt;time&gt;</code></li>
</ul>
<h3>Example of Semantic Page Structure</h3>
<div class="code-example">
<pre><code>&lt;header&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;CodeVerse Library&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;/header&gt;

    &lt;nav&gt;
        &lt;a href="index.html"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;a href="library.html"&gt;Library&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/nav&gt;

    &lt;main&gt;

        &lt;section&gt;
            &lt;h2&gt;Featured Books&lt;/h2&gt;

            &lt;article&gt;
                &lt;h3&gt;HTML Fundamentals&lt;/h3&gt;
                &lt;p&gt;Learn the basics of HTML.&lt;/p&gt;
            &lt;/article&gt;

        &lt;/section&gt;

        &lt;aside&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Related Topics&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;CSS and JavaScript&lt;/p&gt;
        &lt;/aside&gt;

    &lt;/main&gt;

    &lt;footer&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;CodeVerse Digital Library&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/footer&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
<p>
            Using semantic elements helps describe the
            structure of a webpage without depending
            entirely on generic containers.
        </p>
</section>
